put('1007','p3',S('a','proof','A nondegenerate Gaussian posterior is maximized at θ=mN because its negative log density differs from (θ-mN)ᵀSN⁻¹(θ-mN)/2 by a constant. Linearity of expectation gives E[f(x)|data]=φ(x)ᵀmN.','The MAP function equals the BLR posterior mean function.','φ,mN:6×1; if the posterior is degenerate, its Gaussian mean still determines the mean function, with mode interpreted on its support.'),S('b','qualitative','A Gaussian linear image has f(x)|data~N(φᵀmN,φᵀSNφ). Narrow and wide bands therefore express smaller and larger posterior credible ranges for the latent function at each x. The supplied SVG shows narrower bounds near training points and wider bounds toward the ends.','Inner and outer pointwise uncertainty bands about the posterior mean function.','The figure depicts function uncertainty; adding observation noise would increase the variance by its noise variance.',unresolved=['The packet does not assign numerical posterior coverage levels to the two bands. 67% and 95% cannot be confirmed for this specific plot from the provided conditions or SVG.']),S('c','qualitative','The posterior distribution on θ induces a distribution on entire functions. A representative sample curve is generated with θi~N(mN,SN), then fi(x)=φ(x)ᵀθi at all x. Overlaying these illustrates plausible functions and their variation after observing data.','The natural posterior-function interpretation is several posterior parameter draws evaluated as complete curves.','Each complete curve uses one shared θi. The supplied SVG only renders panel (b).',unresolved=['Panel (c) itself and an explicit statement of its sampling construction are not supplied; the posterior-sample reading is the standard interpretation, not visually verified provenance.']))
