put('1007','p8',S('main','proof','Let H=A⁻¹+B⁻¹, h=A⁻¹a+B⁻¹b and k=aᵀA⁻¹a+bᵀB⁻¹b. Expanding both exponents gives xᵀHx-2hᵀx+k. Set C=H⁻¹ and c=Ch, so Hc=h. Then xᵀHx-2hᵀx+k=(x-c)ᵀH(x-c)+k-cᵀHc. The final two terms are independent of x, so normalizing the product leaves Gaussian N(c,C).','C=(A⁻¹+B⁻¹)⁻¹; c=C(A⁻¹a+B⁻¹b).','C is D×D and positive definite, c:D×1. Normalized density is (2π)^(-D/2)|C|^(-1/2) exp[-(x-c)ᵀC⁻¹(x-c)/2]. No commutativity of A and B was used.'))
put('1012','p1',S('a','exact','A=[[2,0],[1,3]], det A=2·3-0·1=6.','Area=6.','Absolute determinant gives nonnegative area.'),S('b','exact','A=[[2,4],[1,2]], det A=4-4=0. Moreover g=2b, so all vertices lie on one line.','Area=0.','The zero area agrees with linearly dependent columns.'))
put('1012','p2',S('a','exact','det Σ=4·10-2·2=36≠0.','det Σ=36; inverse exists.','Positive first minor and determinant also show Σ≻0.'),S('b','exact','Swap diagonal entries, negate off-diagonal entries, and divide by determinant.','Σ⁻¹=(1/36)[[10,-2],[-2,4]].','Σ[[10,-2],[-2,4]]=[[36,0],[0,36]], giving identity after division.'))
put('1012','p3',S('main','proof','The inverse transformation is x=A⁻¹y. The derivative ∂x/∂y=A⁻¹ has absolute determinant 1/|ad-bc|. Substitute x into the original exponent: xᵀx=yᵀA⁻ᵀA⁻¹y. Probability conservation fY(y)dy=fX(A⁻¹y)dx supplies the determinant factor.','fY(y)=[2π|ad-bc|]⁻¹ exp[-yᵀA⁻ᵀA⁻¹y/2], y∈R². Equivalently N(0,AAᵀ).','|AAᵀ|=(det A)² and (AAᵀ)⁻¹=A⁻ᵀA⁻¹. Substitution y=Ax makes its integral equal the original normalized integral 1, including orientation reversing det A<0.'))
put('1012','p4',S('main','exact','x1=y1/3,x2=y2/2 and |det ∂x/∂y|=1/6. The squared norm becomes y1²/9+y2²/4.','fY(y)=1/(12π) exp[-(y1²/9+y2²/4)/2]; fY(0)=1/(12π).','Covariance diag(9,4) has determinant 36; normalizer is 1/(2π√36). Two one-dimensional Gaussian integrals yield total mass 1.'))
put('1012','p5',S('a','exact','From y2=2x2 get x2=y2/2. From y1=x1+x2 get x1=y1-y2/2.','x=A⁻¹y=[[1,-1/2],[0,1/2]]y.','AA⁻¹=I2.'),S('b','exact','∂x/∂y=[[1,-1/2],[0,1/2]], so determinant is 1·(1/2)-0=1/2.','det Jacobian=1/2; absolute determinant=1/2.','This equals 1/det A, with det A=2.'),S('c','exact','Substitute the inverse coordinates into x1²+x2²=(y1-y2/2)²+(y2/2)²=y1²-y1y2+y2²/2 and multiply density by 1/2.','fY(y)=1/(4π) exp[-(y1²-y1y2+y2²/2)/2].','Σ=AAᵀ=[[2,2],[2,4]], det Σ=4, Σ⁻¹=[[1,-1/2],[-1/2,1/2]]. Its normalizer matches; invertible change of variables proves total integral 1.'))
put('1012','p6',S('main','proof','Let δx=x-μx. Since μy=Aμx+b, δy=Aδx+w. Then Cov(x,y)=E[δx(Aδx+w)ᵀ]=ΣxAᵀ+E[δxwᵀ]. Independence and zero means make the last term zero. Transposing yields Cov(y,x)=AΣx.','Covxy=ΣxAᵀ; Covyx=AΣx.','Shapes D×E and E×D, respectively; they are transposes, not generally equal. Diagonality of Q is unnecessary for this result.'))
put('1012','p7',S('main','symbolic','Mean y is Aμx+b. Center y to Aδx+w; expansion and independent zero-mean noise give Cov(y)=AΣxAᵀ+Q. Stack x and y, placing the supplied cross-covariances into opposite off-diagonal blocks.','(x;y)~N((μx;Aμx+b), [[Σx,ΣxAᵀ],[AΣx,AΣxAᵀ+Q]]).','Mean length D+E, covariance (D+E)×(D+E), symmetric. It equals T diag(Σx,Q)Tᵀ with T=[[I,0],[A,I]], so it is positive semidefinite.'))
put('1012','p8',S('main','symbolic','Let H=(y1,…,yt). Under independent fresh noises, m=Aμt and V=AΣtAᵀ+Q. Next y=Cxt+1+vt+1 has mean Cm, variance CVCᵀ+R and cross covariance Cov(xt+1,yt+1|H)=VCᵀ. Stack these blocks; Gaussianity is supplied.','Under the stated fresh-noise interpretation: N((m;Cm), [[V,VCᵀ],[CV,CVCᵀ+R]]), m=Aμt, V=AΣtAᵀ+Q.','If state dimension d and observation dimension e, A:d×d,C:e×d,Q,V:d×d,R:e×e. The joint block is positive semidefinite by the same triangular transformation construction. μ0,Σ0 are not needed once μt,Σt are supplied.',unresolved=['The phrase i.i.d. Gaussian noises does not explicitly state independence from x0/the state/history. If it means only independence among noise draws, the usual prediction blocks additionally require exogeneity. Also w and v cannot literally be identically distributed across different dimensions or unequal Q,R; the conventional reading is each process independent over time and mutually independent.'],assumptions=['wt and vt+1 are independent of the current state and observation history, and of each other.']))
put('1014','p1',S('a','exact','The three states have equal probability p; normalization 3p=1 gives p=1/3.','P(Z=-1.1)=P(Z=0.3)=P(Z=1.5)=1/3, and 0 elsewhere.','Three nonnegative masses sum to 1.'),S('b','exact','Let the height be h. The support length is 1.6-0.9=0.7=7/10. Normalization h·7/10=1 gives h=10/7.','pX(x)=10/7 for 0.9≤x≤1.6, and 0 otherwise.','Integral=(10/7)(7/10)=1. A density may exceed 1; endpoint values do not affect the integral.'))
put('1014','p2',S('main','proof','θ1x+θ2x²=(μx-x²/2)/σ²=-(x-μ)²/(2σ²)+μ²/(2σ²). The final term is independent of x. The unnormalized integral is exp[μ²/(2σ²)]√(2πσ²), so its reciprocal cancels this factor.','p(x|θ)=(2πσ²)^(-1/2)exp[-(x-μ)²/(2σ²)]=N(μ,σ²).','σ²>0 makes the exponent concave in x and the Gaussian integral finite; normalized integral is 1.'))
