put('hw1','p4',S('a','exact','For a 2×2 matrix take the product of diagonal entries minus the product of off-diagonal entries: 3·4-1·2.','det A=10.','Nonzero determinant agrees with invertibility.'),S('b','exact','det B=1·0-(-2)·3.','det B=6.','The minus sign on B12 changes the subtracted product into +6.'),S('c','exact','A diagonal determinant is the product of its diagonal entries, 2·(-1)·3.','det C=-6.','Determinants can be negative; this is not an area magnitude.'),S('d','exact','First-row cofactor expansion: 1(4·4-6·2)-3(2·4-6·0)+5(2·2-4·0)=4-24+20=0. Sarrus: positive cyclic products 1·4·4+3·6·0+5·2·2=36; negative cyclic products 5·4·0+3·2·4+1·6·2=36; difference 0.','det D=0 by both first-row expansion and Sarrus.','The two independent expansions yield the same scalar; Drow3=2Drow1-Drow2 also proves row dependence.'))
put('hw1','p5',S('a','exact','det A=2·1-1·1=1. The adjugate swaps the diagonal and negates off-diagonal entries.','A⁻¹=[[1,-1],[-1,2]].','A A⁻¹=[[2-1,-2+2],[1-1,-1+2]]=I2.'),S('b','exact','det B=1·1-(-2)·3=7. Divide its adjugate by 7.','B⁻¹=(1/7)[[1,2],[-3,1]].','B[[1,2],[-3,1]]=[[7,0],[0,7]], so multiplying by 1/7 gives I2.'),S('c','exact','C is block diagonal with 2×2 block T=[[2,1],[1,2]] and scalar 2. det T=3, T⁻¹=(1/3)[[2,-1],[-1,2]] and inverse scalar=1/2.','C⁻¹=[[2/3,-1/3,0],[-1/3,2/3,0],[0,0,1/2]].','The first block product is I2 and last product is 1, so CC⁻¹=I3; det C=6≠0.'))
put('hw1','p6',S('main','proof','Compute 2x1-x2=(4,-2,6)ᵀ-(1,1,-2)ᵀ=(3,-3,8)ᵀ=x3.','Linearly dependent: 2x1-x2-x3=0.','The coefficient vector (2,-1,-1) is nonzero; all three coordinate residuals are exactly 0.'))
put('hw1','p7',S('main','exact','Set y=a x1+b x2+c x3. Coordinate equations are a+b+2c=1, a+2b-c=-2, a+3b+c=5. Subtracting first from second and third gives b-3c=-3 and 2b-c=4. Thus c=2,b=3,a=-6.','y=-6x1+3x2+2x3.','Reconstruction=(-6,-6,-6)+(3,6,9)+(4,-2,2)=(1,-2,5). The coefficient matrix determinant=5≠0, so representation is unique.'))
put('hw1','p8',S('main','exact','The columns are (1,1,1)ᵀ and (-1,0,1)ᵀ. Their squared norms are 3 and 2 and their dot product is 0, giving AᵀA=diag(3,2).','AᵀA=[[3,0],[0,2]]; determinant=6; inverse=[[1/3,0],[0,1/2]].','diag(3,2)diag(1/3,1/2)=I2, so the requested multiplication verification holds.'))
put('hw1','p9',S('main','exact','Evaluate φ at -1,0,1 and put each transpose into a row. Multiplying θ gives 1+x+2x² at each input.','Φ=[[1,-1,1],[1,0,0],[1,1,1]]; ŷ=(2,1,4)ᵀ. It is linear regression because fθ(x)=θ0+θ1x+θ2x² is linear in the unknown coefficients.','Φ is 3×3 and θ is 3×1. Nonlinearity in the input x does not introduce products or nonlinear functions of parameters.'))
put('hw1','p10',S('a','symbolic','Differentiate u² and -2u with respect to u and 3v² and 4v with respect to v.','∇f=(2u-2,6v+4)ᵀ; ∇f(1,-1)=(0,-2)ᵀ.','Gradient is 2×1; mixed derivatives are both zero.'),S('b','symbolic','The mixed term 2uv contributes 2v to ∂/∂u and 2u to ∂/∂v.','∇g=(2u+2v-4,2u+6v+2)ᵀ; ∇g(1,-1)=(-4,-2)ᵀ.','Gradient is 2×1; both mixed second derivatives are 2.'),S('c','symbolic','Let r=u+2v-w-3. Chain rule gives ∇h=2r∇r=2r(1,2,-1)ᵀ. At (1,0,-1), r=-1.','∇h=(2r,4r,-2r)ᵀ; evaluated gradient=(-2,-4,2)ᵀ.','Gradient is 3×1; the negative w coefficient reverses the third component sign.'))
put('hw1','p11',S('a','symbolic','Use rows indexed by output coordinates and columns by input coordinates (u,v). The derivatives of u+2v are (1,2); those of 3u-v are (3,-1).','Jf=[[1,2],[3,-1]], also at (1,2).','Two outputs and two inputs require a 2×2 Jacobian; this linear map has constant derivative.'),S('b','symbolic','Differentiate each output: u²+v→(2u,1), uv→(v,u), v²→(0,2v).','Jg=[[2u,1],[v,u],[0,2v]]; Jg(1,2)=[[2,1],[2,1],[0,4]].','Three outputs and two inputs require a 3×2 Jacobian.'))
put('hw1','p12',S('a','symbolic','Expand xᵀQx=3x1²+x1x2+x2x1+2x2² and -2bᵀx=-2x1+2x2.','h=3x1²+2x1x2+2x2²-2x1+2x2; ∂h/∂x1=6x1+2x2-2; ∂h/∂x2=2x1+4x2+2.','The two cross terms combine because scalar multiplication commutes; ∇h=2Qx-2b since Q is symmetric.'),S('b','exact','Set derivatives to zero: 3x1+x2=1, x1+2x2=-1. From x2=1-3x1, substitution gives -5x1=-3.','x1=3/5, x2=-4/5.','Qx=b exactly. Q has positive leading minors 3 and det Q=5, so Hessian 2Q is positive definite and this is also the unique minimum.'))
put('hw1','p13',S('main','exact','det Σ=4-1=3 and Σ⁻¹=(1/3)[[2,-1],[-1,2]]. With a=x1-1,b=x2+1, the exponent is -(a²-ab+b²)/3. Expanding gives a²-ab+b²=x1²-x1x2+x2²-3x1+3x2+3.','pX(x1,x2)=1/(2π√3) exp[-(x1²-x1x2+x2²-3x1+3x2+3)/3], on R².','Σ≻0 since its first leading minor is 2 and determinant is 3. Normalizer follows (2π)^(-2/2)|Σ|^(-1/2), so integral=1; quadratic at μ=(1,-1) is 0.'))
put('hw1','p14',S('main','exact','4z²-8z+7=4(z-1)²+3. Thus integral p=c e^(-3/2)∫exp[-2(z-1)²]dz=c e^(-3/2)√(π/2). Setting it to 1 yields c=e^(3/2)√(2/π). The exponent -2(z-1)² is -(z-1)²/(2·1/4).','c=e^(3/2)√(2/π)>0; mean=1, variance=1/4.','Normalized density becomes √(2/π) exp[-2(z-1)²], whose integral is 1. c alone is not the Gaussian prefactor until the e^(-3/2) term is combined.'))
put('hw1','p15',S('main','symbolic','Each independent residual εn=yn-θ0-θ1xn has density (2πσ²)^(-1/2)exp[-εn²/(2σ²)]. Multiply N densities: prefactors multiply to (2πσ²)^(-N/2), and exponent terms sum.','L(θ0,θ1)=(2πσ²)^(-N/2) exp[-Σn=1ᴺ(yn-θ0-θ1xn)²/(2σ²)].','Inputs are fixed, so this is the joint conditional data density as a function of parameters. σ²>0 ensures a positive finite normalizer.'))
put('hw1','p16',S('main','symbolic','The N-dimensional covariance determinant is (σ²)^N and inverse is I/σ². Taking minus the log of the Gaussian likelihood gives N log(2π)/2+N log(σ²)/2+||y-Φθ||²/(2σ²). The first terms are independent of θ and the SSE coefficient is positive.','-log L(θ)=N/2 log(2πσ²)+(1/(2σ²))||y-Φθ||₂². Therefore argmax L=argmin ||y-Φθ||₂².','Φ:N×K, θ:K×1, y:N×1; the norm is scalar. Since log is strictly increasing and negation reverses order, the optimization equivalence holds even if the minimizer is not unique.'))
put('hw1','p17',S('a','proof','J=(y-Φθ)ᵀ(y-Φθ)=yᵀy-2θᵀΦᵀy+θᵀΦᵀΦθ. Because ΦᵀΦ is symmetric, ∇J=-2Φᵀy+2ΦᵀΦθ. Set to zero: ΦᵀΦθ=Φᵀy. For any nonzero v, vᵀΦᵀΦv=||Φv||²>0 under full column rank, so the matrix is invertible and Hessian 2ΦᵀΦ is positive definite.','θML=(ΦᵀΦ)⁻¹Φᵀy; normal equations ΦᵀΦθML=Φᵀy.','Gradient K×1 and Hessian K×K. Positive definiteness proves the unique global minimum, not merely stationarity.'),S('b','exact','Φ=[[1,-1],[1,0],[1,1]], ΦᵀΦ=diag(3,2), Φᵀy=(7,2)ᵀ. Solve to obtain (7/3,1)ᵀ. Predicted values are (7/3-1,7/3,7/3+1); residuals are (2/3,-4/3,2/3).','θML=(7/3,1)ᵀ; ŷ=(4/3,7/3,10/3)ᵀ; S=4/9+16/9+4/9=8/3.','Φᵀ(y-ŷ)=(0,0)ᵀ exactly, verifying the normal equations.'))
put('hw1','p18',S('main','proof','Bayes gives -log posterior=||y-Φθ||²/(2σ²)+||θ||²/(2b²)+constant independent of θ. Its gradient is (ΦᵀΦθ-Φᵀy)/σ²+θ/b². Multiplication by σ² and setting zero yields (ΦᵀΦ+(σ²/b²)I)θ=Φᵀy. Multiplying the objective by the positive 2σ² gives SSE+(σ²/b²)||θ||².','λ=σ²/b²; θMAP=(ΦᵀΦ+λI_K)⁻¹Φᵀy.','λ>0. For v≠0, vᵀ(ΦᵀΦ+λI)v=||Φv||²+λ||v||²>0, so the inverse exists even without full rank. All coefficients, including the intercept, receive the penalty.'))
put('hw1','p19',S('a','exact','ML solves diag(3,2)θ=(7,2)ᵀ. With σ²=b²=1, λ=1 and MAP solves diag(4,3)θ=(7,2)ᵀ.','θML=(7/3,1)ᵀ; θMAP at b²=1 is (7/4,2/3)ᵀ.','Both systems have nonzero diagonal entries, and substitution verifies both right-hand sides.'),S('b','exact','For b²=1/4, λ=1/(1/4)=4. MAP solves diag(7,6)θ=(7,2)ᵀ. Both coordinates shrink as denominators increase.','θMAP at b²=1/4 is (1,1/3)ᵀ. Coordinatewise 1<7/4<7/3 and 1/3<2/3<1; smaller b² strengthens the zero-centered prior and shrinks both intercept and slope toward zero.','This comparison uses the supplied diagonal Gram matrix; in a general correlated basis shrinkage is most directly characterized along eigenvectors of ΦᵀΦ.'))
put('hw1','p20',S('a','exact','Both columns equal (1,2)ᵀ; each column inner product is 1²+2²=5.','ΦᵀΦ=[[5,5],[5,5]], determinant=25-25=0.','Rank is 1, so the unregularized Gram matrix is singular.'),S('b','exact','Predictions depend only on θ1+θ2: Φθ=(θ1+θ2)(1,2)ᵀ. For A and B the sum is 3.','ΦθA=ΦθB=(3,6)ᵀ=y; both SSE values are 0.','Different parameters can yield identical fits because the columns are dependent.'),S('c','exact','J=5(3-θ1-θ2)²+5(θ1²+θ2²). The gradient equations after dividing by 10 are 2θ1+θ2=3 and θ1+2θ2=3. Subtract to get θ1=θ2, hence both are 1. At this point SSE=5 and penalty=5(1+1)=10.','Unique minimizer (θ1,θ2)=(1,1); minimum J=15.','Hessian=2(ΦᵀΦ+5I)=[[20,10],[10,20]] has eigenvalues 10 and 30>0, proving global uniqueness; the exact gradient at (1,1) is zero.'))
put('hw1','p21',S('a','exact','Sum coordinates: Σx=(1+1+3+3,1+3+3+5)ᵀ=(8,12)ᵀ; divide by 4.','x̄=(2,3)ᵀ.','Centered vectors sum to zero.'),S('b','exact','Centered vectors are (-1,-2),(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2). Their outer products sum to [[4,4],[4,8]]; divide by the specified 4.','S=[[1,1],[1,2]].','Symmetric 2×2 matrix with det=1>0. Denominator is 4 as requested, not the unbiased-estimator denominator 3.'))
put('hw1','p22',S('a','exact','Set μx=(1,2)ᵀ, Σx=[[4,1],[1,2]], A=[[1,2],[-1,1]], b=(1,-2)ᵀ. Since y-μy=A(x-μx), μy=Aμx+b, Σy=AΣxAᵀ and Cxy=ΣxAᵀ. Matrix multiplication gives the following.','μy=(6,-1)ᵀ; Σy=[[16,-1],[-1,4]]; Cxy=[[6,-3],[5,1]].','μy:2×1 and both covariance matrices:2×2. Σy is symmetric while Cxy need not be. det Σx=7 and det A=3 imply det Σy=63, agreeing with 16·4-1.'),S('b','exact','Let u=x1-1,v=x2+1. Exponent is -(u²-2uv+2v²)/2, with precision H=[[1,-1],[-1,2]]. Thus μx=(1,-1)ᵀ and Σx=H⁻¹=[[2,1],[1,1]]. det H=1 and H11=1>0 verify a normalized Gaussian prefactor 1/(2π). For A=[[2,-1],[1,1]], b=(-1,2)ᵀ, use μy=Aμx+b, Σy=AΣxAᵀ, Cxy=ΣxAᵀ.','μx=(1,-1)ᵀ, Σx=[[2,1],[1,1]]; μy=(2,2)ᵀ; Σy=[[5,4],[4,5]]; Cxy=[[3,3],[1,2]].','HΣx=I2. det Σy=9=(det A)²det Σx=3²·1. The supplied density prefactor is correct because det Σx=1.'),S('c','exact','Cov(x1,x2)=E[x1x2]-E[x1]E[x2]=0-(1)(-1)=1, so Σx=[[2,1],[1,3]]. Set A=[[1,1],[1,-1]], e=(1,1)ᵀ, y=Ax+eε. Independence gives μy=A(1,-1)ᵀ, Σy=AΣxAᵀ+eeᵀVar ε, and Cxy=ΣxAᵀ. Before noise, AΣxAᵀ=[[7,-1],[-1,3]]; common noise adds [[1,1],[1,1]].','μy=(0,2)ᵀ; Σy=[[8,0],[0,4]]; Cxy=[[3,1],[4,-2]].','The same ε in both coordinates creates off-diagonal noise covariance 1, canceling the pre-noise covariance -1. Zero final covariance alone does not imply independence because Gaussianity is not given here.'))
put('hw1','p23',S('a','exact','Multiply the two N(θxn,1) data densities.','L(θ)=(2π)⁻¹ exp[-{(2-θ)²+(3-2θ)²}/2].','Two independent unit-variance observations give prefactor (2π)^(-2/2).'),S('b','exact','-log L=log(2π)+(1/2)[5θ²-16θ+13]. Its derivative is 5θ-8, zero at 8/5.','θML=8/5.','Second derivative 5>0 proves the unique minimum of negative log likelihood.'),S('c','exact','Adding prior N(0,1) adds θ²/2 plus a constant to the negative log posterior. Derivative becomes 6θ-8, so θMAP=8/6=4/3. Multiply both estimates by x=3.','θMAP=4/3; ŷML=24/5; ŷMAP=4.','Second derivative 6>0. MAP is closer to prior mean 0: 4/3<8/5.'))
put('hw1','p24',S('a','exact','Multiply three independent N(θ,1) densities.','L(θ)=(2π)^(-3/2) exp[-{(2-θ)²+(3-θ)²+(4-θ)²}/2].','Three unit-variance observations determine the prefactor.'),S('b','exact','-log L=(3/2)log(2π)+(1/2)(3θ²-18θ+29). Differentiation gives 3θ-9.','θML=3.','Second derivative 3>0 ensures a unique optimum.'),S('c','exact','The N(1,1) prior adds (θ-1)²/2 to the negative log posterior, so derivative is (3θ-9)+(θ-1)=4θ-10.','θMAP=5/2; MAP is closer to prior mean 1 because |5/2-1|=3/2<|3-1|=2.','Second derivative 4>0. The estimate (9+1)/(3+1)=5/2 also follows from combining three data precision units and one prior precision unit.'))
put('hw1','p25',S('main','exact','Σ0⁻¹=diag(1,1/2), Σ1⁻¹=(1/3)[[2,-1],[-1,2]]. Their sum H=[[5/3,-1/3],[-1/3,7/6]] has determinant 11/6. The linear term h=Σ0⁻¹μ0+Σ1⁻¹μ1=(1/3,1/3)ᵀ. Completing the square gives covariance C=H⁻¹=(1/11)[[7,2],[2,10]] and mean m=Ch=(3/11,4/11)ᵀ. With u=x1-3/11,v=x2-4/11, (x-m)ᵀH(x-m)=(5/3)u²-(2/3)uv+(7/6)v².','m=(3/11,4/11)ᵀ; C=(1/11)[[7,2],[2,10]]. q(x)=√(11/6)/(2π) exp[-(10u²-4uv+7v²)/12], u=x1-3/11, v=x2-4/11.','HC=I2 and Hm=h exactly. det C=6/11, so normalizer=(2π)^(-1)(det C)^(-1/2)=√(11/6)/(2π). H11=5/3>0 and det H=11/6>0 prove integrability and positive covariance; the normalized Gaussian integral is 1.'))
emit(['hw1'])
